.TH FERRY 1 2026-10-16 "ferry 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ferry \- a very simple HTTPS reverse proxy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ferry
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ cert
.IR key ]
.IR mapping ...
.RB [ \-\-
.IR command ...]
.br
.B ferry
.B \-c
.I file
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ mapping ...]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR command ...]
.br
.B ferry \-t
.RB [ \-c
.IR file ]
.RI [ cert
.IR key ]
.RI [ mapping ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ferry
accepts HTTPS connections and forwards the requests over plain HTTP to an
application on the same machine or elsewhere. It is meant to need no
configuration for most uses: with no certificate it issues one from a local
certificate authority it keeps between runs, it listens on IPv4 and IPv6, it redirects plain HTTP
to HTTPS, and it passes WebSockets through.
.PP
Clients may use HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1. Requests are forwarded to upstreams over
HTTP/1.1, by TCP or a Unix domain socket, and bodies are streamed in both
directions.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I cert key
A PEM certificate chain and its unencrypted PEM private key. The certificate
file should contain the full chain (for Let's Encrypt,
.IR fullchain.pem ).
Give both or neither. Without them, ferry uses its local CA; see
.BR CERTIFICATES .
.TP
.I mapping
.IB listen : upstream \fR,
where
.I listen
is
.I port
or
.IB address : port \fR,
where
.I port
may be
.IB http / https
(such as
.BR 80/443 )
to also listen for plain HTTP on the first port and redirect it to HTTPS on
the second, and
.I upstream
is
.IR port ,
.IB host : port \fR,
.BI http:// host\fR[\fB: port\fR],
or a Unix socket path starting with
.BR / ,
.B ./
or
.BR ../ .
A bare upstream port means
.BR localhost ,
which ferry tries as both ::1 and 127.0.0.1. IPv6 addresses are written in
brackets. Without an address, ferry listens on all interfaces, IPv4 and IPv6.
.RS
.PP
.nf
6005:6006                https on 6005 \-> http://localhost:6006
127.0.0.1:6005:6006      listen on 127.0.0.1 only
6005:10.0.0.5:6006       forward to another host
6005:/run/app.sock       forward to a Unix socket
[::1]:6005:[::1]:6006    IPv6 on both sides
80/443:6006              https on 443, http on 80 redirecting to it
.fi
.RE
.PP
Arguments that look like mappings (containing
.B :
or a bare number) are mappings; the rest are the certificate and key.
.TP
.BI \-\- " command" \fR...
Run
.I command
alongside ferry, typically a development server:
.B ferry 6005:5173 \-\- npm run dev .
The command shares ferry's terminal (stdin, stdout and stderr). Requests
that arrive before their upstream accepts connections are held, for up to 30
seconds, instead of getting an error page; each waits only for its own
upstream. The banner is printed as soon as one upstream accepts
connections, below the command's own startup output, and ferry warns about
any still not listening after 30 seconds. SIGTERM is passed on to
the command, and SIGINT too unless it came from the terminal (where the
command gets it directly). ferry exits when the command does, with its exit
status. Not allowed with
.BR \-d .
Command line only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIfile\fR
Read settings from the TOML file
.IR file .
Command\-line options override it, and mappings on the command line are
added to its
.B [[proxy]]
entries. ferry watches the file and reloads it when it changes; see
.BR RELOADING .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test
Check the configuration and exit. Reports every problem found: errors in the
configuration (with file and line), certificates that cannot be loaded, do
not match their key, or have expired; and, as warnings, certificates
expiring within 14 days, listen ports already in use, and missing upstream
sockets. Has no side effects. Exits 0 if there are no errors.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-config
Print the effective configuration (file, options and defaults combined) as a
TOML config file and exit. The output, saved and loaded with
.BR \-c ,
gives the same configuration.
.TP
.BI \-\-access\-log " file"
Write the access log to
.IR file .
.B \-
means standard output (the default);
.B off
disables it.
.TP
.BI \-\-error\-log " file"
Write the error log to
.IR file .
.B \-
means standard error (the default).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-format " format"
Access log format:
.B combined
(the default; Apache's combined layout followed by the request duration
and request ID),
.B common
(Apache's common log layout), or
.B json
(one object per line). In every format, and in the error log, timestamps are
ISO 8601 in UTC with milliseconds, for example
.BR 2026\-10\-16T03:00:00.123Z .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not print the startup banner or write the access log to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-body " size"
Reject request bodies larger than
.I size
with 413 Payload Too Large. Accepts suffixes K, M and G (binary multiples).
The default is no limit.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-redirect
Do not redirect plain HTTP sent to an HTTPS port; it gets a 400 response
explaining the port expects HTTPS instead. Plain HTTP ports named with
.IB http / https
still redirect: that is all they do.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemonize
Run in the background. Not allowed with a command after
.BR \-\- . ferry checks the configuration, loads certificates and
binds its ports first, so errors are still reported in the terminal and in
the exit status. The banner is printed before ferry detaches. Logs that would
go to the terminal are discarded, and ferry warns about this.
.TP
.BI \-\-pid\-file " file"
Write the process ID to
.I file
and remove it on exit. Overrides
.B pid_file
in the configuration file. ferry refuses to start if the file names a process
that is still running. Works with or without
.BR \-d .
.TP
.BI \-\-completions " shell"
Print a completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
.BR fish ,
.B elvish
or
.B powershell
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of options.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
The file is TOML. All keys are optional except that at least one
.B [[proxy]]
entry (or a mapping on the command line) is needed. Unknown keys are errors.
Every path in the file (certificates, logs, the pid file and Unix socket
upstreams) may be relative, and is resolved against the directory containing
the file. Options that are about how ferry is run rather than what it serves
.RB ( \-d ,
.BR \-q ,
.BR \-t )
are command\-line only.
.PP
.nf
.RS
cert = "/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/fullchain.pem"
key = "/etc/letsencrypt/live/example.com/privkey.pem"
access_log = "/var/log/ferry/access.log"  # "\-", "off" or a path
error_log = "/var/log/ferry/error.log"    # "\-" or a path
log_format = "combined"                   # "common", "combined", "json"
max_body = "10M"
redirect = true
pid_file = "/run/ferry/ferry.pid"

[[proxy]]
listen = "80/443"                 # or 443, "127.0.0.1:443", "[::1]:443"
upstream = 3000                   # or "host:port", "http://host:port",
                                  # or "/run/app.sock"

[[proxy]]
listen = 8443
upstream = "/run/admin.sock"
cert = "admin.pem"                # this listener's own certificate
key = "admin.key"
max_body = "100M"                 # this listener's own limit

[advanced]                        # defaults shown
connect_timeout = "5s"            # connecting to an upstream
handshake_timeout = "15s"         # TLS handshake and the first request
idle_timeout = "90s"              # idle connections, stalled requests
shutdown_grace = "10s"            # in\-flight requests on shutdown
# max_connections = 10000         # default: from the open\-file limit
qr_code = true                    # QR codes in the banner for phones
ca_name_constraints = true        # limit a new local CA to local names
.RE
.fi
.PP
Durations accept ms, s, m and h suffixes or a plain number of seconds.
.PP
ferry raises its open\-file limit as far as it may at startup.
.B max_connections
caps the connections open at once, to clients and upstreams together, and
by default fits within that limit. When it's reached, new clients wait to be
accepted, and a request that needs an upstream connection is answered 503.
.SH CERTIFICATES
When no certificate is configured, ferry uses its own local certificate
authority. The CA is created on first use and kept, with its key, in
.I ~/Library/Application Support/ferry/
on macOS and
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/ferry/
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/ferry/ )
on Linux, as
.I ca.pem
and
.IR ca\-key.pem .
It is valid for 10 years. Trust it once on each device and every ferry on
this machine works there without warnings.
.PP
The CA has name constraints: it can only vouch for localhost, names ending in
.IR .local ,
and loopback, private, link\-local and carrier\-grade NAT addresses. If its
key leaked, it could not be used to impersonate other sites to devices that
trust it (Android ignores name constraints). The server certificate then
covers the machine as
.IR hostname .local
but not as the bare host name. An address it cannot cover, such as a public
LAN address, is left out of the server certificate with a warning. To make a CA without them, set
.B ca_name_constraints = false
in
.B [advanced]
and delete
.I ca.pem
and
.IR ca\-key.pem ;
the setting only applies when the CA is created.
.PP
On every start and reload ferry issues a new server certificate from the CA,
written to
.IR server.pem ,
covering localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, the host name (and
.IR hostname .local)
and the machine's LAN IPv4 addresses. It is reissued while ferry runs when
those change, for example when a laptop joins another network.
.PP
ferry serves a page explaining how to trust the CA on iOS, Android and this
machine, with the commands for the system store and for any Firefox (and, on
Linux, Chrome) NSS databases found. It is plain HTTP on a port of its own,
chosen by the system and kept across reloads, so nothing of ferry's appears
at the applications' addresses; the banner links to it. The CA certificate
itself is at
.I /ca.crt
on that port. If every listener using the CA is bound to loopback, so is the
page. When stderr is a
terminal, the banner shows the start and end of the CA's SHA\-256
fingerprint, a QR code for the
page at the machine's LAN address with the steps beside it, and a QR code
for each application; set
.B qr_code = false
in
.B [advanced]
to leave it out.
.PP
A file named
.I self\-signed.pem
in the same directory is from ferry versions before the local CA and is no
longer used.
.PP
ferry serves the same certificate whatever name the client asks for.
.SH RELOADING
ferry watches the configuration file and every certificate and key file it
uses (without
.BR \-c ,
the certificate files from the command line), checking them once a second.
Saves made by renaming a new file into place and symbolic link changes are
both noticed. When one
changes, or on
.BR SIGHUP ,
ferry loads and checks the configuration as
.B \-t
would. If it is valid it is applied in place: new listeners start, removed
ones stop, and changed upstreams and certificates are used for new
connections, while requests already in progress finish undisturbed. If it is
not valid, the errors are logged and the running configuration is kept.
.SH REQUESTS
ferry forwards the method, path, query, body and headers, keeping the
.B Host
header. It removes hop\-by\-hop headers and any
.B Forwarded
header, sets
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
(appending the client's address, so the last entry is the one to trust),
.BR X\-Real\-IP ,
.BR X\-Forwarded\-Proto ,
.B X\-Forwarded\-Host
and
.BR X\-Forwarded\-Port ,
and adds an
.B X\-Request\-Id
(passed to the upstream, returned to the client, and logged) unless the
client sent one. HTTP/1.1 upgrades such as WebSockets are passed through,
and closed when nothing has moved either way for
.BR idle_timeout ,
or when ferry shuts down.
.PP
Plain HTTP sent to an HTTPS port is redirected (308) to the same URL over
HTTPS. A mapping such as
.B 80/443:3000
also listens for plain HTTP on port 80 and redirects every request to the
same URL over HTTPS on 443; port 80 never serves the application. ferry only
listens on the ports it is given.
.PP
When an upstream cannot be reached, ferry answers 502 with a short
explanation (HTML for browsers, plain text otherwise), or 504 if connecting
timed out or the upstream didn't start answering within
.BR idle_timeout .
Only clients on loopback or a private network are told which upstream failed
and why; others get the request ID, which the error log line has too.
.PP
A connection that sends no request within
.B handshake_timeout
is closed, as is one with no requests in flight for
.BR idle_timeout ,
over HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 alike. So is one where a request has stopped
moving, with no bytes either way for longer than
.BR idle_timeout ;
slow transfers that keep moving are not affected.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop accepting connections and exit once in\-flight requests finish, or
after
.I shutdown_grace
(10 seconds by default). A second
.B SIGINT
exits immediately.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration and reopen log files.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reopen log files, for use after log rotation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FERRY_LOG
Error log level: one of
.BR error ,
.BR warn ,
.BR info ,
.B debug
or
.BR trace .
The default is
.BR info .
Any other value is ignored with a warning.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
If set, output is never coloured.
.TP
.BR HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the local CA is kept.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on a clean shutdown or a successful
.BR \-t ;
1 on errors in the configuration or at startup; 2 for invalid usage. When
ferry runs a command, it exits with the command's status: its exit code, or
128 plus the number of the signal that killed it.
.SH EXAMPLES
HTTPS for a development server, with a certificate from ferry's local CA:
.PP
.RS
ferry 5173:5173
.RE
.PP
A production certificate and two applications:
.PP
.RS
ferry fullchain.pem privkey.pem 443:3000 8443:/run/admin.sock
.RE
.PP
Turn a command line into a config file, check it, then run it in the
background:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ferry \-\-print\-config 443:3000 \-\-max\-body 10M > ferry.toml
ferry \-t \-c ferry.toml
ferry \-d \-\-pid\-file ferry.pid \-\-access\-log access.log \e
      \-\-error\-log error.log \-c ferry.toml
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR security (1),
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR launchd.plist (5)
